// ──────────────────────────── types ──────────────────────────────────────

record RequestInfo(string Method, string Path, IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> Query, IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> Headers, string? Body);

/// <summary>Given: what may be logged as it is, and what marks a value that was hidden.</summary>
static class LogRules
{
    public const string Redacted = "[Redacted]";
    public static readonly string[] AllowedHeaders = { "Accept", "Content-Type", "User-Agent", "X-Correlation-Id" };
    public static readonly string[] SecretQueryKeys = { "access_token", "api_key", "code", "password", "token" };
    public const string EmailPattern = @"[^/@\s]+@[^/@\s]+";
}

drills.explanation:
Two case-insensitive sets decide everything, and every value either passes through them or becomes the marker:

```csharp
var allowed = new HashSet<string>(LogRules.AllowedHeaders, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
var secret = new HashSet<string>(LogRules.SecretQueryKeys, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
foreach (var (name, value) in request.Headers) headers[name] = allowed.Contains(name) ? value : LogRules.Redacted;
foreach (var (name, value) in request.Query) query[name] = secret.Contains(name) ? LogRules.Redacted : value;
// Path: Regex.Replace(request.Path, LogRules.EmailPattern, "[email]"); BodyLength: request.Body?.Length ?? 0
```

The deny-list from the prompt, hiding only `Authorization` and `Cookie`, passes the sign-in test but not "a new credential header such as X-Api-Key is redacted too": the partner's key comes out in clear text. Building a set without `StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase` misses `content-type` in the casing test and `Access_Token` in the query test. Dropping unknown headers instead of marking them loses the evidence that they were sent, and the `[Redacted]` test sees two missing keys. Adding the body, even just once for debugging, puts `hunter2` back into the fields.
